{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"pwd && sed -n '1,240p' docs/design/datom.world.md && git status --short && git rev-parse --short HEAD\"","aggregated_output":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-php-p4\n---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n?? collab/1793496000000-engineer-p4-checkpointB-fold.gpt-6-luna.findings.md\n?? collab/1793500000000-engineer-p4-objects-defects.gpt-6-luna.findings.md\n?? collab/1793506000000-engineer-p4-heap-fixture.gpt-6-luna.findings.md\n?? collab/1793515000000-engineer-p4-leak-fix.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\n?? collab/1793540000000-engineer-p4-hang.gpt-6-luna.findings.md\n?? collab/1793550000000-engineer-p4-retier.gpt-6-luna.findings.md\n?? collab/1793560000000-engineer-p4-foreach016.gpt-6-luna.findings.md\n?? collab/1793660000000-engineer-p4-yangcall-conformance.gpt-6-luna.findings.md\n?? collab/1793700000000-engineer-p4-foreign-admission.gpt-6-luna.findings.md\nedc50134\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' docs/agents/build-n-test.md && rg -n \\\"refusals-are-named|phpt-selection-runs|gc_013|quick selection\\\" test src resources collab\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' docs/agents/build-n-test.md && rg -n \\\"refusals-are-named|phpt-selection-runs|gc_013|quick selection\\\" test src resources collab\"","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\n\n```sh\nbb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\nbb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\nbb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\nbb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\nbb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\nbb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\nbb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\nbb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\nbb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\n```\n\n`test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\nthe JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\n`--changed FILE...` or `--base REF` uses `affected/select` and\n`affected/lane-tests`, exactly as `test:changed:clj` does. Wide input selects\nthe full JVM set here. It never changes `test:changed`'s selection or exit-2\nsemantics. `--workers N` must be positive (default 4 on the laptop).\n\nShard membership comes from every entry in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`, using\n`affected/select-subsystems` and the same longest-prefix ownership rule as\n`test:sub`. As in the shiva script below, currently unowned namespaces run\nin an explicit `unmapped` residual shard, printed by name. Registry omissions\ntherefore cannot silently shrink coverage. Each nonempty shard gets its own\n`clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns> ...` process, with at most N JVMs running.\nPrerequisites are derived from the entire selected JVM reach and finish\nserially before workers start; the Node REPL build prerequisite\n(`build:yin-repl-node`) also ensures `bb deps:npm`.\n\nEach invocation writes a separate `target/profile/quick-sharded-<id>/`:\n`selected.txt`, `ns-<shard>.txt`, `covered.txt`, `uncovered.txt`, per-shard logs,\nand `summary.edn`. Before execution it checks **set equality** between selected\nnamespaces and the union of shard lists, plus absence of duplicate assignments.\nAny uncovered, unexpected, or duplicate namespace fails loudly before tests.\nThe final summary totals tests, assertions, failures and errors and separates\nJVM wall time from total time including selection/builds. A nonzero child exit,\nfailure/error, or missing final test-runner summary makes the task exit nonzero.\nDry plans write namespace evidence but execute no builds or tests. An empty\nselection prints `0 JVM namespaces selected` and exits 1, including dry plans.\nThe final `summary.edn` includes a `:verification-scope` record; the same EDN\nrecord is printed on stdout with `:type :verification-scope`, `:runner`,\n`:scope`, `:exit`, `:dry-run?`, and `:gate-complete?`. JVM feedback always has\n`:scope :jvm-feedback` and `:gate-complete? false`, even on exit 0.\n\n`test:routed <changed-files...>` applies this priority table to the whole change\nset, prints the exact dispatch, executes it and propagates its result:\n\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Change class (first match wins)                                                              | Dispatch                                                              |\n+==============================================================================================+=======================================================================+\n| Existing wide inputs (`deps.edn`, `bb.edn`, `src/dev/`, ANTLR, package locks, runner         | `bb test` — full quick tri-host; success required                     |\n| configs, etc.), toolchain manifests, CI workflows, build scripts                             |                                                                       |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Only `.md`, `.txt`, `.rst` documentation, excluding test resources                           | `git diff --check HEAD -- <paths>` — documentation whitespace/format  |\n|                                                                                              | lint only                                                             |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Foundation code owned by a layer-0 entry in `subsystems.edn`, or `yang.clojure`, `yang.php`, | `bb test:quick-sharded --changed <paths>` — broad selected JVM        |\n| `yang.typescript`, `yang.python` code                                                        | feedback                                                              |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Other code/tests/fixtures with a nonempty affected selection                                 | `bb test:changed --changed <paths>` — existing quick tri-host         |\n|                                                                                              | selection                                                             |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Other non-doc input with no statically selected tests                                        | `bb test` — fail closed rather than report a vacuous green gate       |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nMixed changes take the widest matching route. Missing paths, an empty git change\nset, unknown flags, and the wrong cwd fail. Deleted paths are validated against\nthe base tree. If the leaf command returns exit 2, routing immediately dispatches\n`bb test` and requires its success. `--dry-run`/`--list` only reports the route;\nits zero exit is **not** a verification result. Documentation checks inspect\nstaged and unstaged changes together against HEAD. Paths absent from\n`git ls-files` print `unchecked (untracked)` and appear in the scope record\nunder `:unchecked`; they cannot complete the docs gate. These checks do not lint\nexecutable examples or validate documentation links.\n\nRouting prints its own final EDN verification-scope record. `:gate-complete?`\nmeans the dispatched gate completed successfully, not that every landing gate\nis satisfied. It is false for foundation/JVM feedback, dry runs, unchecked docs,\nand failed dispatches. Successful checked docs, selected tri-host, and full\nquick tri-host routes report `:docs-whitespace`, `:selected-quick-tri-host`, and\n`:full-quick-tri-host` respectively. Exit codes continue to propagate child\nresults, so automation must inspect the scope as well as the exit code.\n\n**Gate requirements are unchanged.** Sharded JVM success alone is not G2/G3\ntri-host success: for foundation/compiler changes also run\n`bb test:changed:cljs --changed <paths>` and\n`bb test:changed:cljd --changed <paths>`, respecting the existing JVM/Dart\nserialization rule. G1, full post-merge `bb test` (G3), applicable slow G4,\ndemo smoke checks and review/landing gates all remain required. Node and Dart\nlanes retain their current commands. The original unsharded tasks remain\navailable. Do not run another lane set concurrently on the laptop.\n\n#### Worktree-local prerequisite caches\n\n`bb gen:antlr` now checks a content key over all grammar/manifests (including\nrecorded helper/generated digests and runtime pins), the generator/cache source,\n`deps.edn`, `mise.toml`, actual Java/javac versions and canonical worktree path.\nIt also verifies the names and SHA-256 bytes of all generated targets, compiled\nclasses and cached upstream helpers before a hit. Changed inputs or missing,\nremoved or corrupted outputs trigger a full generation, retaining the generator's\ndigest checks. Filtered/discovery invocations retain their CLI behavior and\ninvalidate the complete-build stamp.\n\n`bb deps:npm` wraps `npm ci` with a key covering `package-lock.json`,\n`package.json`, npm configuration, Node/npm versions, platform, relevant npm/Node\nenvironment and worktree identity; it verifies the installed file tree before\nreuse. A failed command never establishes a cache hit. Existing Node build tasks\nensure this dependency too. Direct `npm ci` is still available.\n\nStamps and fail-fast writer locks live under `target/prereq-cache/`. Remove a\nlock only after confirming its owning process died. Symlinked output parents\nthat escape the worktree are refused. This is reuse **within one worktree**,\nnot a shared artifact store: never share mutable `build/`, `lib/`, `target/` or\n`node_modules/` across live worktrees. ANTLR generation and Dart compilation\nstill require the existing worktree isolation/serialization rules; the cache\ndoes not make overlapping builds and tests safe. Peer executables and shadow\nbuilds themselves are not cached by this mechanism.\n\nLaptop pilot (2026-10-29, 8 cores, 4 workers, `yang.clojure.cljc` changed):\nthe unchanged selector and the sharded plan both covered **61 JVM namespaces**;\nboth completed with **955 tests, 14,848 assertions, zero failures/errors**.\nSharded JVM wall time was **177.165 s** (total including prerequisites **257.207 s**;\nouter command **258.27 s**). The original `test:changed:clj` command completed\nin **376.79 s** overall and reported **298 s** for its JVM lane (that runner\nrounds to whole seconds). The investigation's earlier unsharded command had\ntimed out at 300 s without a summary. These are single-run observations, not\na median/p95 claim or a replacement for tri-host landing verification.\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\nrg: resources: No such file or directory (os error 2)\ncollab/1793550000000-engineer-p4-retier.gpt-6-luna.findings.md:3:Applied the architect's re-tier-now ruling. No guest source, iteration limit, or expected output was changed. The PHPT corpus marks expensive entries with `:slow true`; the combined quick selection test excludes those entries, and each moved fixture has an individual `^:slow` test so slow-tier selection remains explicit and one case can be run directly.\ncollab/1793550000000-engineer-p4-retier.gpt-6-luna.findings.md:8:- `Zend/tests/gc_013.phpt` (10,001 iterations)\ncollab/1793550000000-engineer-p4-retier.gpt-6-luna.findings.md:26:The quick selection still does not complete locally. A progress-isolated run reaches `Zend/tests/foreach_016.phpt` (quick entry 195) and has not advanced for over two minutes; running that source alone through `run-walker` also exceeded a 20-second timeout. The source iterates a three-element array by reference and calls `array_unshift` once when `$v == 2`; PHP's pinned expectation is finite. This is not one of the finite stress fixtures, so I left it in quick and did not disguise it by re-tiering. It appears to be a separate PHP array/foreach engine behavior defect, outside this re-tier-only authorization.\ncollab/1793550000000-engineer-p4-retier.gpt-6-luna.findings.md:28:The full `yang.php.antlr.e2e-test` quick namespace did not complete under 360 seconds, and the PHPT quick selection did not finish under 600 seconds. The measured progress points to the `foreach_016` source as the remaining blocker. The re-tier change is committed separately below; the quick-tier completion gate remains open for architect/engine follow-up.\ncollab/1793660000000-engineer-p4-yangcall-conformance.gpt-6-luna.findings.md:17:The PHP e2e namespace run excluding `:slow` tests completed with 19 tests, 1,111 assertions, 3 failures, and 0 errors. The two new conformance tests passed. Existing failures were the quick php-src selection (271 entries: 108 pass, 48 unsupported, 115 fail) and two expectations in `refusals-are-named-test` for `<?php echo FOO;` (missing diagnostic). An earlier unfiltered run, started before the closure entry change, also reported the `Zend/tests/gc_023.phpt` slow stress case failing and was stopped before completion.\ncollab/1793515000000-engineer-p4-leak-fix.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:245:The JVM quick selection includes 280 active PHPT entries out of 1,088 recorded\ncollab/1793515000000-engineer-p4-leak-fix.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:249:confirmed the process was actively executing `phpt-selection-runs-test`\ncollab/1793515000000-engineer-p4-leak-fix.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:257:(59 min 35 sec)** in `phpt-selection-runs-test`, with no aggregate test result.\ncollab/1793515000000-engineer-p4-leak-fix.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:272:exclusion and only the already-timed-out `phpt-selection-runs-test` omitted\ncollab/1793515000000-engineer-p4-leak-fix.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:303:`yang.php.antlr.e2e-test/refusals-are-named-test` (`e2e_test.clj:103-104`),\ncollab/1793540000000-engineer-p4-hang.gpt-6-luna.findings.md:19:`phpt_selection.edn` has 1,088 entries. The actual `phpt-selection-runs-test` did not finish under a 300-second wall-clock timeout. The largest selected finite loop I isolated was `Zend/tests/gc_013.phpt`: it runs `for ($i = 0; $i < 10001; $i++)`, took 258,911 ms through the walker, then reached an unsupported `gc_collect_cycles()` call. The similar `Zend/tests/gc_023.phpt` has two explicit finite bounds (9,999 and 10,001); the combined stress probe was still processing it when its 300-second wrapper expired. Other large loop cases found by source scan either have explicit numeric bounds or are marked `:needs :p4` and excluded from this selection.\ncollab/1793540000000-engineer-p4-hang.gpt-6-luna.findings.md:31:- `phpt-selection-runs-test`: did not complete within 300 seconds.\ncollab/1793700000000-engineer-p4-foreign-admission.gpt-6-luna.findings.md:42:## `gc_013` triage\ncollab/1793700000000-engineer-p4-foreign-admission.gpt-6-luna.findings.md:53:I did not run `gc_013` to completion against a PHP CLI oracle in this worktree:\ntest/yang/php/antlr/e2e_test.clj:219:(deftest refusals-are-named-test\ntest/yang/php/antlr/e2e_test.clj:494:(deftest phpt-selection-runs-test\ntest/yang/php/antlr/e2e_test.clj:497:      (run-phpt-selection \"php-src quick selection:\" entries))\ntest/yang/php/antlr/e2e_test.clj:513:  (run-phpt-stress-case \"Zend/tests/gc_013.phpt\"))\ntest/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn:6057:  {:id \"Zend/tests/gc_013.phpt\",\ntest/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn:6058:   :path \"Zend/tests/gc_013.phpt\",\n","exit_code":2,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '200,255p' test/yang/php/antlr/e2e_test.clj; sed -n '450,525p' test/yang/php/antlr/e2e_test.clj; sed -n '1,160p' test/yang/php/antlr/e2e_test.clj; sed -n '1,160p' collab/1793700000000-engineer-p4-foreign-admission.gpt-6-luna.findings.md; rg -n \\\"shiva|docker.*oracle|oracle.*docker|php:8\\\\.3\\\\.35\\\" scripts dev collab docs -g '*.md' -g '*.sh' -g '*.clj'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '200,255p' test/yang/php/antlr/e2e_test.clj; sed -n '450,525p' test/yang/php/antlr/e2e_test.clj; sed -n '1,160p' test/yang/php/antlr/e2e_test.clj; sed -n '1,160p' collab/1793700000000-engineer-p4-foreign-admission.gpt-6-luna.findings.md; rg -n \\\"shiva|docker.*oracle|oracle.*docker|php:8\\\\.3\\\\.35\\\" scripts dev collab docs -g '*.md' -g '*.sh' -g '*.clj'\"","aggregated_output":"    (is (= programs/equal-matrix eq))\n    (is (= programs/spaceship-matrix sp))\n    (testing \"cells reasoned from the manual, independent of the golden\"\n      (let [idx (zipmap programs/matrix-values (range))]\n        (doseq [[a b eq? cmp] programs/known-cells\n                :let [i (idx a), j (idx b)]]\n          (is (= (if eq? \\1 \\0) (get-in eq [i j])) (str a \" == \" b))\n          (is (= (get {-1 \\<, 0 \\=, 1 \\>} cmp) (get-in sp [i j])) (str a \" <=> \" b)))))))\n\n\n(deftest float-text-test\n  (let [src (str \"<?php\\n\"\n                 (apply str (for [[lit] programs/float-cases]\n                              (str \"echo \" lit \", \\\"|\\\"; var_dump(\" lit \");\\n\"))))\n        expected (str (apply str (for [[_ echo dump] programs/float-cases]\n                                   (str echo \"|float(\" dump \")\\n\"))))]\n    (is (= expected (h/run-walker src)))))\n\n\n(deftest refusals-are-named-test\n  (doseq [[source construct]\n          [[\"<?php $a = &f();\" :reference-source]\n           [\"<?php $$a[0] = 1;\" :assignment-target]\n           [\"<?php function f(int ...$a) {}\" :typed-variadic]\n           [\"<?php function f(&...$a) {}\" :variadic-reference]\n           [\"<?php $a = &new B;\" :reference]\n           [\"<?php static $a = 1;\" :static]\n           [\"<?php echo FOO;\" :constant]\n           [\"<?php echo \\\"a$b[0]\\\";\" :interpolation]\n           [\"<?php echo \\\"a$b->c\\\";\" :interpolation]\n           [\"<?php echo (array)1;\" :cast/array]\n           [\"<?php $o = new class { public $x = 1; };\" :anonymous-class]]\n          :when construct]\n    (let [{:keys [diagnostics]} (h/run-php source)\n          d (first diagnostics)]\n      (is (= :yang.php/unsupported (:yang.php/diagnostic d)) source)\n      (is (= construct (:construct d)) source))))\n\n\n(deftest leading-numeric-string-warns-test\n  (let [r (h/run-raw \"<?php echo \\\"5 apples\\\" + 1;\")]\n    (is (= \"6\" (:php/out r)))\n    (is (= [{:php.diag/warning true,\n             :errno 2,\n             :message \"A non-numeric value encountered\"}]\n           (:php/diag r)))\n    (is (nil? (:php/uncaught r))))\n  (testing \"a well formed numeric string does not warn\"\n    (is (= [] (:php/diag (h/run-raw \"<?php echo \\\" 5 \\\" + 1;\"))))))\n\n\n(deftest pow-fractional-refusal-test\n  (testing \"G-pow-frac: a fractional exponent refuses at run time, with the ledger id\"\n    (let [r (h/run-raw \"<?php echo 2 ** 0.5;\")]\n      (is (= \"Error\" (-> r :php/uncaught :class)))\n      (is (= \"** with a fractional exponent is not supported yet (yang.php known gap G-pow-frac)\"\n  (let [text \"--TEST--\\nt\\n--FILE--\\n<?php echo 1;\\n--EXPECTF--\\nint(%d) %s\\n\"\n        sections (phpt/parse-phpt text)]\n    (is (= \"t\" (get sections \"TEST\")))\n    (is (= \"<?php echo 1;\" (get sections \"FILE\")))\n    (is (phpt/matches-expect? sections \"int(42) abc\\n\"))\n    (is (not (phpt/matches-expect? sections \"int(x) abc\")))\n    (is (phpt/matches-expect? (phpt/parse-phpt \"--FILE--\\nx\\n--EXPECT--\\nhi\\n\") \"hi\\n\")))\n  (testing \"classification\"\n    (is (= :select (:status (phpt/classify {\"FILE\" \"<?php function f(&$x){} f($a); echo count([]);\",\n                                            \"EXPECT\" \"x\"}))))\n    (is (= :p4 (:needs (phpt/classify {\"FILE\" \"<?php class A {} new A;\", \"EXPECT\" \"x\"}))))\n    (is (= :unsupported-surface\n           (:reason (phpt/classify {\"FILE\" \"<?php preg_match('/a/', 'a');\", \"EXPECT\" \"x\"}))))\n    (is (= :unknown-functions\n           (:reason (phpt/classify {\"FILE\" \"<?php mystery_fn(1);\", \"EXPECT\" \"x\"}))))\n    (is (= :no-expectation (:reason (phpt/classify {\"FILE\" \"<?php echo 1;\"})))))\n  (testing \"path predicates\"\n    (is (phpt/selected-path? \"Zend/tests/list/list_001.phpt\"))\n    (is (phpt/selected-path? \"ext/standard/tests/array/current_variation1.phpt\"))\n    (is (not (phpt/selected-path? \"ext/standard/tests/strings/sprintf_basic1.phpt\")))))\n\n\n(defn- run-phpt-selection\n  [label entries]\n  (let [results (for [{:keys [id source expect expectf]} entries\n                      :let [r (engine-result source)]]\n                  (cond\n                    (:refused r) [:unsupported id]\n                    (:threw r) [:fail id]\n                    (phpt/matches-expect?\n                      (cond-> {} expect (assoc \"EXPECT\" expect) expectf (assoc \"EXPECTF\" expectf))\n                      (str (:out r)\n                           (when (:uncaught r)\n                             (str \"Fatal error: Uncaught \" (:class (:uncaught r)) \": \"\n                                  (:message (:uncaught r))))))\n                    [:pass id]\n                    :else [:fail id]))\n        by (group-by first results)]\n    (println label (count entries) \"entries;\"\n             \"pass\" (count (:pass by)) \"unsupported\" (count (:unsupported by))\n             \"fail\" (count (:fail by)))\n    (is (empty? (:fail by)) (pr-str (take 5 (:fail by))))))\n\n\n(deftest phpt-selection-runs-test\n  (if-let [sel (read-edn-file \"test/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn\")]\n    (let [entries (remove #(or (:needs %) (:slow %)) (:entries sel))]\n      (run-phpt-selection \"php-src quick selection:\" entries))\n    (println \"php-src selection: phpt_selection.edn not generated yet (run yang.php.phpt on shiva)\")))\n\n\n(defn- run-phpt-stress-case\n  [id]\n  (if-let [sel (read-edn-file \"test/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn\")]\n    (if-let [entry (first (filter #(= id (:id %)) (:entries sel)))]\n      (do\n        (is (:slow entry) (str id \" must stay in the slow tier\"))\n        (run-phpt-selection (str \"php-src slow stress \" id \":\") [entry]))\n      (is false (str \"missing PHPT stress case \" id)))\n    (println \"php-src selection: phpt_selection.edn not generated yet (run yang.php.phpt on shiva)\")))\n\n\n(deftest ^:slow phpt-gc-013-stress-test\n  (run-phpt-stress-case \"Zend/tests/gc_013.phpt\"))\n\n\n(deftest ^:slow phpt-gc-023-stress-test\n  (run-phpt-stress-case \"Zend/tests/gc_023.phpt\"))\n\n\n(deftest ^:slow phpt-bug39438-stress-test\n  (run-phpt-stress-case \"Zend/tests/bug39438.phpt\"))\n\n\n(deftest ^:slow phpt-bug40261-stress-test\n  (run-phpt-stress-case \"Zend/tests/bug40261.phpt\"))\n(ns yang.php.antlr.e2e-test\n  \"End to end on the JVM: source -> CST packet -> lowering stage -> program\n   envelope -> the bundled program on each of the four VMs (the AST walker,\n   the semantic, stack and register VMs). Every fixture of\n   `yang.php.antlr.programs` prints the same text on all four.\"\n  (:require\n    [clojure.walk :as walk]\n    [clojure.edn :as edn]\n    [clojure.java.io :as io]\n    [clojure.string :as str]\n    [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\n    [yang.core.uast :as u]\n    [yang.php.antlr.cst :as cst]\n    [yang.php.antlr.e2e-harness :as h]\n    [yang.php.antlr.portable-harness :as php-h]\n    [yang.php.antlr.packets :as packets]\n    [yang.php.antlr.packets-p3b :as packets-p3b]\n    [yang.php.antlr.permutations :as perms]\n    [yang.php.antlr.phpt :as phpt]\n    [yang.php.antlr.programs :as programs]\n    [yang.php.antlr.programs-p3b :as p3b]\n    [yang.typescript.antlr.e2e-test :as ts]\n    [yin.vm :as vm]\n    [yin.vm.test-utils :as tu]))\n\n\n(def ^:private vms [:ast-walker :semantic :stack :register])\n\n\n(defn- rendered\n  \"The text `fixture` prints: `:out`, then the fatal line of an `:uncaught`.\"\n  [{:keys [out uncaught]}]\n  (str out\n       (when uncaught\n         (str \"PHP Fatal error:  Uncaught \" (:class uncaught) \": \" (:message uncaught)))))\n\n\n(deftest ^:slow fixtures-on-every-vm-test\n  (doseq [{:keys [id source] :as fixture} programs/fixtures\n          :let [out (rendered fixture)]]\n    (testing (name id)\n      (let [results (h/run-php source)]\n        (is (not (contains? results :diagnostics)) (pr-str results))\n        (doseq [k vms]\n          (is (= out (get results k)) (str (name id) \" on \" k)))))))\n\n\n(deftest fixtures-on-the-walker-test\n  (doseq [{:keys [id source out diag uncaught oracle]} programs/fixtures\n          :let [r (h/run-raw source)]]\n    (is (not= :pending oracle) (name id))\n    (is (= out (h/latin1->text (:php/out r))) (name id))\n    (is (= (vec diag) (mapv (juxt :errno :message) (:php/diag r))) (name id))\n    (is (= uncaught (some-> (:php/uncaught r) (select-keys [:class :message])))\n        (name id))))\n\n\n(defn- app?\n  [node op]\n  (and (map? node)\n       (= :application (:type node))\n       (= {:type :variable, :name op} (:operator node))))\n\n\n(defn- lowered-ts\n  [source]\n  (let [{:keys [program diagnostics]} (ts/lower-source source :bundled)]\n    (assert (nil? diagnostics) (pr-str diagnostics))\n    (let [{:yin/keys [batch root]} program]\n      (nth batch root))))\n\n\n(defn- export-projection\n  \"The donor program followed by its explicitly named global export read.\"\n  [donor export-name]\n  (u/then donor (u/sexp->uast (list 'js/global-get {:js/str export-name}))))\n\n\n(defn- install-php-function-export\n  \"Project `%foreign` into PHP's function namespace before the source body.\n   The lowered PHP call and its resolve/call nodes remain untouched.\"\n  [ast export-name]\n  (let [projection (u/sexp->uast\n                     (list 'cell/set! 'php.rt/functions\n                           (list 'assoc\n                                 '(cell/get php.rt/functions)\n                                 export-name\n                                 '%foreign)))\n        seen (volatile! false)\n        result (walk/postwalk\n                 (fn [node]\n                   (if (app? node 'php/run-main)\n                     (let [body (first (:operands node))]\n                       (assert (= :lambda (:type body)) \"php/run-main body is not a lambda\")\n                       (vreset! seen true)\n                       (assoc node :operands [(update body :body #(u/then projection %))]))\n                     node))\n                 ast)]\n    (assert @seen \"PHP program has no php/run-main application\")\n    result))\n\n\n(defn- run-with-ts-export\n  [donor-source export-name php-source]\n  (let [donor (lowered-ts donor-source)\n        {:keys [ast diagnostics]} (h/lower-program php-source)\n        _ (assert (nil? diagnostics) (pr-str diagnostics))\n        php-ast (install-php-function-export ast export-name)\n        composition {:type :application,\n                     :operator {:type :lambda,\n                                :params ['%foreign],\n                                :body php-ast},\n                     :operands [(export-projection donor export-name)]}\n        result (vm/value (vm/eval (tu/create-vm {:modules (php-h/host-registry)})\n                                  composition))]\n    (h/render result)))\n\n\n(defn- run-with-ts-global\n  \"Project `%foreign` into a PHP global before the unmodified source body.\"\n  [donor-source export-name php-global php-source]\n  (let [donor (lowered-ts donor-source)\n        {:keys [ast diagnostics]} (h/lower-program php-source)\n        _ (assert (nil? diagnostics) (pr-str diagnostics))\n        global-projection (u/sexp->uast\n                            (list 'php/global-store! php-global '%foreign))\n        seen (volatile! false)\n        php-ast (walk/postwalk\n                  (fn [node]\n                    (if (app? node 'php/run-main)\n                      (let [body (first (:operands node))]\n                        (assert (= :lambda (:type body)) \"php/run-main body is not a lambda\")\n                        (vreset! seen true)\n                        (assoc node :operands [(update body :body #(u/then global-projection %))]))\n                      node))\n                  ast)\n        _ (assert @seen \"PHP program has no php/run-main application\")\n        composition {:type :application,\n                     :operator {:type :lambda,\n                                :params ['%foreign],\n                                :body php-ast},\n                     :operands [(export-projection donor export-name)]}\n        result (vm/value (vm/eval (tu/create-vm {:modules (php-h/host-registry)})\n                                  composition))]\n    (h/render result)))\n\n\n(deftest real-lowered-ts-export-called-from-php-test\n  (testing \"unmodified PHP source calls a published TS global export through yang.call\"\n    (is (= \"4\"\n           (run-with-ts-export\n             \"globalThis.donor = (x, y) => x + y;\"\n             \"donor\"\n             \"<?php echo donor(1.5, 2.5);\")))))\n\n\n(deftest real-lowered-ts-callback-called-from-php-test\n  (testing \"PHP stores a foreign function cell in an array, invokes it, and keeps its identity\"\n    (is (= \"same\"\n           (run-with-ts-global\n# PHP admission of foreign `yang.call` cells\n\n## Implementation\n\n- PHP's bundled prelude now includes the shared `yang.call` definitions.\n- `php/resolve-fn` admits only function-table values that pass\n  `yang.call/eligible?`; non-conforming values retain the undefined-function\n  error.\n- PHP callable checks and resolution recognize an existing shared entry\n  structurally. `php/call`, named calls, and binding-profile calls now route\n  through `yang.call/invoke` / `invoke-with`.\n- PHP's linked program requires `yang.call` before `php`, and the PHP module\n  pins its `yang.call` content address. The linked harness publishes and names\n  both modules.\n- Strict identity compares a shared callable with itself before PHP's local\n  type interpretation. This lets a TS function cell survive PHP global and\n  array storage and pass `===` without interpreting its foreign value layout.\n- Added a real lowered TS-to-PHP test: the TS cell is placed in a PHP global,\n  stored in a PHP array, called from PHP source, and checked for strict\n  identity. The TS callback returns `undefined`; its result is deliberately\n  discarded because cross-language value interpretation is outside this\n  admission change.\n\n## Verification\n\n- New callback test, direct TS-export PHP call, bundled-prelude closure check,\n  and PHP module-spec test: pass on JVM.\n- Linked-profile equivalence test: pass on semantic, stack, and register VMs.\n- PHP class-model, objects, and generators namespaces: 14 tests / 1,201\n  assertions pass.\n- `cljstyle check`, clj-kondo, and `git diff --check`: clean.\n- The broad fast PHP e2e + prelude run is not green on this checkout. It reports\n  the existing PHPT quick-selection mismatches (271 entries: 108 pass, 48\n  unsupported, 115 fail) and the existing `echo FOO` refusal expectation.\n  Its first pass also found that PHP's declared host names omitted `data/nth`,\n  which the newly bundled shared helper uses; that manifest omission is fixed,\n  and the closed-prelude test passes on rerun. The added callback checks pass.\n  A prior checkout finding\n  (`collab/1793660000000-engineer-p4-yangcall-conformance.gpt-6-luna.findings.md`)\n  recorded the same quick-selection and `echo FOO` failures before this change.\n\n## `gc_013` triage\n\nThe pinned PHPT entry carries the canonical source and `EXPECT` body\n`bool(true)\\nok`. `phpt/matches-expect?` compares the observed output to that\nbody after trimming trailing whitespace, and the stress runner reports\n`:fail` only when execution throws or the output mismatches. The harness\ncomparison therefore appears consistent with the pin. The likely issue is\nengine behavior: this checkout also reports the nearby `gc_023` stress case\nas a mismatch, and the PHP GC cycle collector is not implemented by the PHP\nruntime here.\n\nI did not run `gc_013` to completion against a PHP CLI oracle in this worktree:\nno `php` executable is installed, and prior work measured its 10,001-loop\nwalker path at roughly 259 seconds before it reached the unsupported GC call.\nTreat the expected output as authoritative; verify against the pinned PHP\nCLI on shiva before changing the expectation or harness.\nrg: scripts: No such file or directory (os error 2)\nrg: dev: No such file or directory (os error 2)\ncollab/1793700000000-engineer-p4-foreign-admission.gpt-6-luna.findings.md:57:CLI on shiva before changing the expectation or harness.\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:118:`test:sub`. As in the shiva script below, currently unowned namespaces run\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:342:| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:345:| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:431:## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:439:| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Server snapshot: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1. The repo's `mise.toml` selects Flutter `latest` on beta, not a fixed version. Activate the repo toolchain before running any lane. |\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:443:that is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:456:ssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:474:export PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:491:avoidable\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:510:The first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:528:  sit far inside jupiter's 48 cores / 62 GB and shiva's 24 / 62;\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:608:- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:620:- **The nightly full suite** (`~/ci/nightly-full.sh` on shiva, tmux\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:656:  shiva, tmux session `nightly`, scheduled 03:00 daily through the\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:690:laptop — route Node lanes to the laptop or shiva); **Dart quick tier\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:707:### Remote agent execution (delegate CLIs on shiva and jupiter)\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:744:Known state: `claude` and `codex` verified working on shiva (2026-10-24, the\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:59:| jolt J0c is probe-only: no lifecycle/close/reset, unavailable-helper behavior, or enforced input/nesting/time budgets; unary/indent stack limits are censored measurements, not safe bounds; cancellation unsupported; TSan not run and LSan has no positive control; the FFI cleanup cannot recover when no pointer is obtainable or the release primitive itself fails (injected frees that throw show release attempts, not reclamation); no production or frontend-readiness claim | astra J0c re-check 1792590000000; J0c r5/r6 reports | J5 integration seat (jolt) | closed 2026-10-27 in J5 (jolt-j5): the wrapper enforces a 4 MiB input bound (-6) and a 256-level nesting bound by a flat prescan (-7), clamped by the thread's stack, refused before any ANTLR object exists as `:parser-budget` (spec §7.6); the stage owns its worker (reset remakes it, close disposes it, closed workers refuse); a missing or wrong-layout library answers `:unavailable-parser` and a later selection retries; evidence `bb probe:jolt-j5` (below/at/above per axis, 10x the old crash depths in process, the old crash depths in child processes refusing, the fault-injection harness property), the J0c workers/cycles/sanitize legs, `bb test:jolt-j5`, both platforms (J5 findings `1793098786624`); the limits that survive are the next row. Platform qualification (owner waiver \"linux only\"): the corrected crash-depth driver, the -4 parity/sanitizer legs and the shell corpus of that closure ran on Linux x86_64 only (shiva, jupiter); the earlier macOS arm64 results predate those fixes. REOPENED 2026-10-28: the codex review of e4d43e5e found four prescan bypasses (a CR/form-feed-ended comment and backslash-space joins the prescan misread, uncounted comprehension `for` chains, unary runs discarded inside brackets, and flat binary chains whose deep trees the recursive walkers overflowed), and the Architect ruling replaced the prescan rather than repairing it (slice J5b). Closing evidence 2026-10-28 in J5b; the row closes with J5 when the codex re-review of J5b signs off (pending): the prescan is deleted; guards inside recognition refuse as status -7 `nesting <m> <b> rule|lookahead` before any buffer exists: 8,192 live rule frames (the frame depth is the length of the current context's parent chain, which equals the live rule activations; recorded per context at virtual rule entry), 4,096 SLL lookahead links and 256 full-LL links past the outer context (on every RULE transition of prediction); both walkers are iterative; recognition, export and worker disposal run on a wrapper-owned 16 MiB thread (spec §7.6). Evidence: `bb probe:jolt-j5` on macOS arm64 (the review's repros refusing three times then parsing `x * 6`; at/over per shape exactly at the predicted n; 10x the old crash depths; the old crash depths in children on the jolt thread; a 20,000-deep `1+` tree equal to the JVM's packet), `bb calibrate:jolt-j5` (every bound at most a quarter of the parse stack per shape, worst 1.6 MB), the lifecycle and accessor legs; Linux x86_64 (shiva 2026-10-29), all at the round-3b bytes: `bb probe:jolt-j5` 